Go beyond unitary mapping ([3](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E3 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) to consider quantum channel mapping ([4](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E4 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")).

We use them to take into account two kinds of probabilities: probabilistic mixture of states and states superposition. For the latter, probability is the square of the sum of amplitudes, while for the former, probability is the sum of squared amplitudes. Both effects are present in real life data. There are a number of studies suggesting a limitation of unitary scalarization [Kendall et al. 2018](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#bib.bib30); [Sener and Koltun 2018](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#bib.bib31), the other reevaluate recent research [Kurin et al. 2022](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#bib.bib32). It should be noted how researchers commonly use unitarity — they take a vector, normalize its L2L^{2} norm, and consider the squared Euclidean projections obtained as if they were probabilities. Existing approaches do not distinguish probabilistic mixture of states from their superposition. This work is trying to overcome this deficiency by using input data in the form of density matrices ([7](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E7 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) and mapping them with a general quantum channel ([4](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E4 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")). Consider a few demonstrations.

