## III A Unitary Mapping of Mixed States

In quantum channel learning there are two sources of mixed states. First, the original input density matrices ([7](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E7 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) can be in a mixed state. Secondly, the quantum channel itself, through the transformation ([4](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E4 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")), can create mixed states. In this section we consider the first source. Let the data be of mixed states according to mapping ([7](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E7 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")), while the quantum channel itself is assumed to be unitary ([3](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E3 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) or possibly partially unitary with D≤nD\leq n. To apply our theory and software we need to obtain a tensor Sj​k;j′​k′S\_{jk;j^{\prime}k^{\prime}} with which to solve the optimization problem ([23](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E23 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")), now with Ns=1N\_{s}=1.

