This expansion is similar to the eigenvector expansion ([39](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S4.E39 "In IV A Hierarchy of Unitary Operators ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")), but ket |⋅⟩\Ket{\cdot} and bra ⟨⋅|\Bra{\cdot} are now operators, and SS is called a [superoperator](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superoperator). Note that usual orthogonality does not hold ⟨𝒰[s]|𝒰[s′]⟩≠δs​s′\Braket{\mathcal{U}^{[s]}\big.|\mathcal{U}^{[s^{\prime}]}}\neq\delta\_{ss^{\prime}}, ([51](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S4.E51 "In IV A Hierarchy of Unitary Operators ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) follows from the partial unitarity constraints ([28](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S3.E28 "In III A Unitary Mapping of Mixed States ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")), and ([52](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S4.E52 "In IV A Hierarchy of Unitary Operators ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) follows from the hierarchy constraints ([45](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S4.E45 "In IV A Hierarchy of Unitary Operators ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")). By continuously solving (s=0,1,2,…s=0,1,2,\dots) the optimization problem ([27](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S3.E27 "In III A Unitary Mapping of Mixed States ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) subject to the constraints ([28](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S3.E28 "In III A Unitary Mapping of Mixed States ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) and ([45](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S4.E45 "In IV A Hierarchy of Unitary Operators ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) we can build the required hierarchy of solutions (λi​j[s],uj​k[s])\left(\lambda^{[s]}\_{ij},u^{[s]}\_{jk}\right) to reconstruct the quantum channel from it, see com/polytechnik/kgo/KGOHierarchy.java for an implementation. A possible issue with the constraint ([45](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S4.E45 "In IV A Hierarchy of Unitary Operators ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) is that for the solution ss in the hierarchy, Eq. ([43](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S4.E43 "In IV A Hierarchy of Unitary Operators ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) is not satisfied for some projections, the number of which equals the number of previous states in the hierarchy; for the ground state s=0s=0, all projections are satisfied. However, the main advantage of ([45](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S4.E45 "In IV A Hierarchy of Unitary Operators ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) is that the matrix ([52](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S4.E52 "In IV A Hierarchy of Unitary Operators ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) for an approximated SS from ([49](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S4.E49 "In IV A Hierarchy of Unitary Operators ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) with the full Ns=D​nN\_{s}=Dn basis is equal to the corresponding matrix with the exact SS. For the reconstruction of a quantum channel as a mixed unitary channel, a few solutions with high ℱ[s]\mathcal{F}^{[s]} may be sufficient in a number of practical ML problems. The obtained hierarchy may have up to D​nDn solutions, which is the maximum possible number NsN\_{s} of terms in the Kraus sum ([4](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E4 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")).

