### IV.1 A Demonstration of Constructed Hierarchy of Operators

Let us demonstrate the construction of operators hierarchy. First, we would like to mention that this problem is more difficult for numerical solution than the original ([27](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S3.E27 "In III A Unitary Mapping of Mixed States ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")). With the “external” constraints ([45](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S4.E45 "In IV A Hierarchy of Unitary Operators ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) added, we were able to solve the problem with NsN\_{s} not greater than 55 to 1010, depending on the values of DD and nn. The first difficulty arises from numerical instability and the need for a new [adjustment algorithm](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#A1.E73 "In A.2 An Adjustment of Operators to Orthogonal ‣ Appendix A Numerical Solution ‣ Quantum Channel Learning") to satisfy both the ([28](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S3.E28 "In III A Unitary Mapping of Mixed States ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) and ([45](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S4.E45 "In IV A Hierarchy of Unitary Operators ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) constraints, however this problem is technical and can be resolved with a little effort. The second difficulty is a significant one. The optimization problem we are able to solve is a quadratic functional ([16](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E16 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) optimization. However, mixed states fidelity ([11](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E11 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) is not of this form, thus it cannot be directly applied to a general quantum channel. In the unitary case where Ns=1N\_{s}=1, there are several good options, the most noticeable being Fϱ​ρF^{\sqrt{\varrho}\sqrt{\rho}} ([31](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S3.E31 "In III A Unitary Mapping of Mixed States ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")). In cases where Ns>1N\_{s}>1, there are no good options, as depicted in Fig.[1](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S3.F1 "Figure 1 ‣ III.2 A Demonstration Of Density Matrix Square Root Mapping ‣ III A Unitary Mapping of Mixed States ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")b. For this reason we try a number of “proxies” for total fidelity ([8](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E8 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")). Specifically Fϱ​σF^{\varrho\sigma} ([10](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E10 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")), FvF^{v} ([14](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E14 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")), FN​ϱ2F^{N\varrho^{2}} ([15](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E15 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) and Fϱ​ρF^{\sqrt{\varrho}\sqrt{\rho}} ([31](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S3.E31 "In III A Unitary Mapping of Mixed States ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")). These four expressions are quadratic in 𝒰\mathcal{U} what allows us to build a Sj​k;j′​k′S\_{jk;j^{\prime}k^{\prime}} to which the optimization technique can be applied.

