A quantum channel is always constructed with a preset mapping. The constraints ([5](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E5 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) mean that a unit matrix from the Hilbert space *IN* should be mapped to a unit matrix in the Hilbert space *OUT*. In [Malyshkin 2022](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#bib.bib10); [Belov and Malyshkin 2024](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#bib.bib11) a quantum channel converting the Gram matrix in *IN* into the Gram matrix in *OUT* was considered, with a regularization it is equivalent to the same ([5](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E5 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")). In Section [III](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S3 "III A Unitary Mapping of Mixed States ‣ Quantum Channel Learning") a quantum channel mapping ρ→ϱ\sqrt{\rho}\to\sqrt{\varrho} was considered, in the Ns=1N\_{s}=1 unitary case the same quantum channel maps both ρ\rho and ρ\sqrt{\rho} what allowed us to apply unitary learning to density matrix mappings. The problem of constructing a good quadratic proxy-fidelity in the case of Ns>1N\_{s}>1 is a subject for future research.

