## Appendix A Numerical Solution

The described numerical algorithm is a further development of the one presented in [Belov and Malyshkin 2024](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#bib.bib11). The advancements over the previous version include:

* • The ability to simultaneously include quadratic constraints of different types, such as ([24](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E24 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) and ([25](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E25 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")), or alternatively, ([54](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S4.E54 "In IV.2 Trace Preserving Maps ‣ IV A Hierarchy of Unitary Operators ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) and ([25](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E25 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")), requires the introduction of two kinds of Lagrange multipliers: λi​j\lambda\_{ij} and νs​s′\nu\_{ss^{\prime}}. These multipliers are obtained from the solution of the linear system ([81](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#A1.E81 "In A.4 Lagrange Multipliers Calculation ‣ Appendix A Numerical Solution ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")). * • Different groups of quadratic constraints create different convergence-helper linear constraints. A general method, which does not involve the use of a special basis as in [Belov and Malyshkin 2024](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#bib.bib11), is formulated in Appendix [A.5](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#A1.SS5 "A.5 Convergence-Helper Linear Constraints ‣ Appendix A Numerical Solution ‣ Quantum Channel Learning"). This method involves varying the total NcN\_{c} quadratic constraints to obtain Nc−1N\_{c}-1 homogeneous linear constraints and a single “simplified” (partial) quadratic constraint ([26](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E26 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")), which facilitates the use of a standard eigenvalue problem solver as an iterative algorithm building block. * • In addition to the convergence-helper constraints, we can now include “external” linear constraints. These external constraints can be used to construct a hierarchy of solutions for the novel algebraic problem ([43](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S4.E43 "In IV A Hierarchy of Unitary Operators ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")). * • Now that we have several types of constraints, especially “external” and convergence-helper constraints, the procedure for adjusting the solution to satisfy all constraints becomes more complicated. This is because adjustments for different groups of constraints may conflict with each other. Therefore, a conflict resolution strategy is required, such as the one discussed in Appendix [A.2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#A1.SS2 "A.2 An Adjustment of Operators to Orthogonal ‣ Appendix A Numerical Solution ‣ Quantum Channel Learning"). * • The algorithm described below converges only when Ns=1N\_{s}=1: maximize ([27](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S3.E27 "In III A Unitary Mapping of Mixed States ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) subject to the constraints ([28](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S3.E28 "In III A Unitary Mapping of Mixed States ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")). Currently, it does not work for a general quantum channel where Ns>1N\_{s}>1, as the problem becomes degenerate and the algorithm fails to converge. However, the calculations below are presented for an arbitrary NsN\_{s} because the technique provides a clear approach for adding constraints of various types. The current version of the algorithm converges only for unitary (and partially unitary) learning when Ns=1N\_{s}=1, with the “external” constraints possibly used to build a [hierarchy of unitary operators](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S4 "IV A Hierarchy of Unitary Operators ‣ Quantum Channel Learning").

The ability to simultaneously include quadratic constraints of different types, such as ([24](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E24 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) and ([25](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E25 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")), or alternatively, ([54](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S4.E54 "In IV.2 Trace Preserving Maps ‣ IV A Hierarchy of Unitary Operators ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) and ([25](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E25 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")), requires the introduction of two kinds of Lagrange multipliers: λi​j\lambda\_{ij} and νs​s′\nu\_{ss^{\prime}}. These multipliers are obtained from the solution of the linear system ([81](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#A1.E81 "In A.4 Lagrange Multipliers Calculation ‣ Appendix A Numerical Solution ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")).

