The selection of Kraus operators BsB\_{s} in which to evaluate ([23](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E23 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) is nonunique, there should be a gauge that regulates redundant degrees of freedom. For example consider data of exact unitary mapping |ψl⟩​⟨ψl|→|ϕl⟩​⟨ϕl|\Ket{\psi\_{l}}\Bra{\psi\_{l}}\to\Ket{\phi\_{l}}\Bra{\phi\_{l}} with an operator 𝒰\mathcal{U} providing perfect coverage in ([2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E2 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")). Let we want to describe this data with ([4](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E4 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) mapping of Kraus rank Ns=2N\_{s}=2. Then any combination Bs=ws​𝒰B\_{s}=w\_{s}\mathcal{U} with 1=∑s|ws|21=\sum\_{s}|w\_{s}|^{2} is a solution. The problem becomes degenerate. This creates difficulty in both analyzing the results and achieving convergence in the iterative algorithm – any degeneracy greatly degrades it. It is common to consider Kraus operators BsB\_{s} in the canonical form ([6](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E6 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")). The problem becomes: converting a given bs,j​kb\_{s,jk} matrix to the canonical form of Eq. ([25](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E25 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")).

