### A.5 Convergence-Helper Linear Constraints

The problem we study involves quadratic constraints ([24](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E24 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) and ([25](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E25 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")). These constraints cannot be directly incorporated into the eigenproblem ([59](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#A1.E59 "In Appendix A Numerical Solution ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")). However, homogeneous linear constraints can be easily incorporated. Let’s construct the linear constraints corresponding to the constrained local variation of the current iteration bs,j​kb\_{s,jk}. The concept can be illustrated by varying a quadratic constraint ⟨𝐱|𝐲⟩=0\Braket{\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{y}}=0 to obtain a linear constraint on δ​𝐱\delta\mathbf{x} and δ​𝐲\delta\mathbf{y} at fixed 𝐱=uj∗\mathbf{x}=u\_{j\*} and 𝐲=uj′∗\mathbf{y}=u\_{j^{\prime}\*}: δ​⟨𝐱|𝐲⟩=⟨δ​𝐱|𝐲⟩+⟨𝐱|δ​𝐲⟩=0\delta\Braket{\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{y}}=\Braket{\delta\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{y}}+\Braket{\mathbf{x}|\delta\mathbf{y}}=0; similarly, two expressions 1=⟨𝐱|𝐱⟩=⟨𝐲|𝐲⟩1=\Braket{\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}}=\Braket{\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{y}} are replaced by ⟨𝐱|𝐱⟩−⟨𝐲|𝐲⟩=0\Braket{\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}}-\Braket{\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{y}}=0. By varying it, obtain a linear constraint on δ​𝐱\delta\mathbf{x} and δ​𝐲\delta\mathbf{y}: ⟨δ​𝐱|𝐱⟩+⟨𝐱|δ​𝐱⟩−⟨δ​𝐲|𝐲⟩−⟨𝐲|δ​𝐲⟩=0\Braket{\delta\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{x}}+\Braket{\mathbf{x}|\delta\mathbf{x}}-\Braket{\delta\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{y}}-\Braket{\mathbf{y}|\delta\mathbf{y}}=0. In the case of unitary mapping (D=nD=n, Ns=1N\_{s}=1), this corresponds to replacing the n⁡(n+1)/2n(n+1)/2 quadratic constraints of the unitary property ([28](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S3.E28 "In III A Unitary Mapping of Mixed States ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) with n⁡(n+1)/2−1n(n+1)/2-1 homogeneous linear constraints ([60](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#A1.E60 "In Appendix A Numerical Solution ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")), that simply reduce the search space dimension ([62](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#A1.E62 "In Appendix A Numerical Solution ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")), and a single quadratic constraint ([26](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E26 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")), referred to as a simplified (partial) constraint. This problem can then be iteratively solved with excellent convergence using an eigenvalue problem solver as a building block.

