## Appendix C Parametrizing states with memory using a feedback loop

In this and previous works, we considered a [memoryless quantum channel](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_channel#Memoryless_quantum_channel) described by a set of Kraus operators BsB\_{s} or, in the simplest case where Ns=1N\_{s}=1, by a single unitary operator 𝒰\mathcal{U}. A question arises about how memory can be introduced into the model. Modeling a system with even a single bit of internal state is a daunting task, see Appendix H of [Malyshkin 2019](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#bib.bib20), where we discuss the problem of modeling a [synchronous positive-edge-triggered D flip-flop](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flip-flop_(electronics)#Classical_positive-edge-triggered_D_flip-flop) (D trigger) with ML. Any memory can be represented by a feedback loop, as seen in, for example, a D-trigger or a [recurrent neural network](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recurrent_neural_network). The question is how to introduce memory into the quantum channel ([3](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.04406#S2.E3 "In II Formulation of the Problem ‣ Quantum Channel Learning")) described by an operator 𝒰\mathcal{U}? For pure states, the channel transforms an input state |ψ⟩\Ket{\psi} into the output state |𝒰|ψ⟩\Ket{\mathcal{U}|\psi}. Let’s assume there is a feedback loop that requires some (but not all) components of the output state to be equal (within a phase) to the same components of the input state.

